Z||000000||Mattyman||09-15-2001||02:02 PM||||I have an 87 Coupe and just the other day i went out to take it for a spin and neither of the headlights came up.  They lights both worked but there seemed to be no power going to the motors.  They were working fine when i last used them then i had it parked at school for a week and went to drive at night and no motor motion. It has to be something electrical because i took it to a place about 4 weeks ago to fix my oil pressure gause and it was doing some weird things after that, like the tach would just quit for like 2 seconds, that happened about 5 times.  I don't really know what to check and i need to get them back in working order. If anyone can help i'd appreciate it. Thanks||||reg||11
Z||000001||Raydar||09-15-2001||02:19 PM||||Could be the switch or the headlight control module. Once you flip the light switch on, flip it off and on quickly. See if they don't come up. My Formula is doing the same thing, and it's getting worse. In addition, the lights don't always go down, now.<BR>Once I figure mine out, I'll let you know.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||09-15-2001||04:04 PM||||I guess you really need to determine if you have two problems -- the tach and the headlight motors -- or if you have one electrical problem common to both.  For the tach, I would remove the back cover of the instrument panel and make sure that the connectors are plugged in tightly.  If it is a separate problem, I would guess that your headlight motor controller has gone south.  You can pick them up from 87+ Firebirds if you can't find an 87 or 88 Fiero in a junk yard.  They use the same module.  (In the Firebirds they are mounted on the front firewall about where the Fiero's windshield wiper motor is.)  I have no schematics for the headlight module to see what fuse controls it, etc.  It is possible, I guess, that you simply have a blown fuse....<P>If you don't know, the headlight module is under the front driver side fender.  Remove the 10 mm screws along the top of the fender, the 7mm screws along the lower part of the fender (in the fender well) and the side marker light (torx head screws).  There are a couple more 7mm screws under the marker to remove and you can pull the fender out of the way to get to the module.  Be careful to not try and pull the fender completely off since it is still connected behind the rocker panel.  The module is basically behind the marker light, a black box 1/2" thick and about 5"x5" square with two connectors.  I bought one from the yard for $3.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 09-15-2001).]||||reg||1
